- Story & Context: Jora, the ship's lead engineer, stands before the main reactor core and admits that she redirected the fuel meant for deceleration to power her private speaker arrays. As she speaks, the heat of the core warms her skin, but she feels the icy vibration of the floor panels rattling through her boots. She cries out in the dialect of the ship-born, her voice echoing into the intake fans: "Skift the pulse, my sins hum louder than the engine's ghost." 
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A cinematic silhouette of a woman standing before a pulsing, iridescent reactor core, with geometric light patterns reflected in her glass faceplate.

Track 3: The Clockmaker’s Funeral at Proxima
- Story & Context: Upon reaching the Proxima orbital gate, the crew realizes the Clockmaker—the entity that maintained their internal rhythm—has ceased functioning entirely, leaving them in a state of eternal, motionless quiet. A single, solitary trumpet blast rings out through the vacuum-sealed observation deck, marking the end of their measurement of time. The ship begins to drift into the unknown, its metal frame moaning in the absence of human instruction, confirming that "the watch face fell when the orbit forgot our name." 
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A golden mechanical clock mechanism floating in the darkness of deep space, shattered into thousands of tiny, glowing shards amidst a backdrop of nebular gas.

